Definition: An inline function is expanded in-line at the point of call by compiler, avoiding call/return jump overhead.

Advantages:
1. Faster execution speed by eliminating function call stack overhead.
2. Better performance for small, frequently called helper functions.

Limitations:
- Increases program binary size (code bloat) if function is large.
- Compilers ignore inline request if function contains loops, switch, static variables, or recursion.

Example Program:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
inline int cube(int x) {
    return x * x * x;
}
int main() {
    cout << "Cube of 5 = " << cube(5) << endl;
    return 0;
}
